his is a  sign of hope at least  so who has committed to what in this  deal uh julia  well the the actual text of the  agreement has still not been made  available as we speak but  we do know um that the plan provides for  ukrainian vessels to escort or guard  cargo ships through mined water so the  waters will not be specifically demined  wire the black sea out of ukrainian  territorial waters towards  turkey both sides have made promises  that they would not attack these ships  now there is also going to be a  coordination center to be set up here in  istanbul at this uh  strategically very important black sea  location uh staffed by representatives  of russia ukraine and turkey to oversee  the grain exports and also to conduct  inspections of these ships that was a  specific russian demand that all ships  cargo ships traveling to ukraine or  coming back would be inspected to make  sure there are no weapons or military  equipment on board but ukraine said they  wanted uh that to take place in turkish  water so uh we're assuming right now  that's exactly how it's gonna look like  right because trust has been a big issue  uh in these talks for both sides  uh well that that is of course true and  i've just you know mentioned a few  points and there's obviously very  complex agreement but for the ukrainians  security has always been a top priority  and they said they would not approve  anything that does not guarantee the  safety of their southern regions and the  security of their of the exports of  their agricultural uh  products and ukraine as we all know has  accused russia in recent weeks of uh you  know um specifically uh targeting and  more frequently targeting ukrainian  cities so ukraine has always been  warning that you know they they can  trust russian promises specifically on  security and i think that will remain a  sticking point  thank you for that uh julie at yulia han  in istanbul  we'll take a look at this from a  different angle with david labord wh