ies  in the region yes but i will not say  it's a totally game changer about how  russia will behave on the ground uh and  they may still hurt  ukrainian agricultural infrastructure  for instance right now of course the  important thing is to get the grain onto  world markets and into shops  we don't know i suppose how long it's  going to take to to actually set this  deal up and actually get get working  before the grain can actually reach  people who need it  yes exactly i think the implementation  is going to be crucial  because what we really also need is an  agreement at scale if um let's say in  one month we just managed to get one or  two ships out of ukraine  um that's more or less i would say  nothing you know what we need is uh to  be able to have 20 30  ships a day that can go in and out of  ukraine if really we want to get  something that will be  close to normal  good talking to you thank you for  joining us david labord from the  international food policy research  institute  thank you  you
 

